            <ArticleTitle>Migrated disc in the lumbar spinal canal--case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>693-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 49-year-old man who had complained of back pain for 20 years presented with numbness and pain in his left leg persisting for 6 weeks. Magnetic resonance imaging demonstrated a peripherally enhanced intraspinal mass at the L-3 level. The mass was completely removed. The operative and histological findings revealed degenerated disc fragments surrounded by granulation tissue. His symptoms were completely relieved. Migrated disc should be included in the differential diagnosis of patients with a long history of back pain and an intraspinal mass.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
